<DataTemplate xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
              xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
              xmlns:vs="http://schemas.microsoft.com/visualstudio/extensibility/2022/xaml"
              xmlns:wv2="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Web.WebView2.Wpf;assembly=Microsoft.Web.WebView2.Wpf"
              xmlns:colors="clr-namespace:Microsoft.VisualStudio.PlatformUI;assembly=Microsoft.VisualStudio.Shell.15.0">
  <!--
    Remote UI XAML is parsed inside the Visual Studio process, so it can only reference assemblies
    loadable there. Visual Studio ships WebView2 internally, which is why the WebView2 control can
    be referenced by assembly name (see vs2026/README.md, open question #1: this renders, but the
    extension has no code reference to the control for two-way postMessage).

    EXPERIMENT RESULT (tested on real VS 2026): with CreationProperties removed, this XAML parses
    and the tool window opens, but the WebView2 stays blank — the control cannot initialize because
    its default user-data folder would be created next to devenv.exe (not writable), and an
    out-of-proc extension cannot configure the WebView2 environment inside the VS process.
    Remote UI + WebView2 is therefore RULED OUT; the Seeky UI now runs in SeekyModalWindow
    (own-process WPF + WebView2). These tool window classes are kept only as documentation.

    __SEEKY_INDEX_URI__ is substituted at runtime by SeekyToolWindowContent.GetXamlAsync.
  -->
  <Grid Background="{DynamicResource {x:Static colors:EnvironmentColors.ToolWindowBackgroundBrushKey}}">
    <wv2:WebView2 Source="__SEEKY_INDEX_URI__" />
  </Grid>
</DataTemplate>
